# ---- the fort and the French Lines ----
N('fort-ticonderoga','Fort Ticonderoga (Fort Carillon)','Ticonderoga','adk','fort','standing',43.84167,-73.3875,'site','open','drive','forts','1755–1777',
  'The French built Fort Carillon between October 1755 and 1757 on the narrows at the south end of Lake Champlain, where the La Chute portage from Lake George comes down; the name Ticonderoga comes from the Mohawk for "it is at the junction of two waterways". In 1758 about 4,000 French held it against some 16,000 British; the British took it in 1759 and renamed it, and in 1775 Ethan Allen and Benedict Arnold captured it for the Americans. Left to fall into ruin and stripped by local people for its stone, it was bought in 1820 by William Ferris Pell, and the Pell family had it restored, opening it to the public in 1909. The Fort Ticonderoga Association, founded in 1931, runs it as a museum and research centre.',
  'The restored stone star fort with its barracks, the powder magazine rebuilt in 2008, and the King\'s Garden north of the fort, reconstructed by Sarah Pell from 1921 and restored in 1995. Wikipedia says some walls and the 19th-century Pell pavilion have been in poor condition; the fort has been on the National Historic Landmark watch list since 1998. The museum fills the barracks (contents not checked).',
  'The fort that changed hands four times, rebuilt by the family that bought its ruins.',
  'At 102 Fort Ti Road, run by the Fort Ticonderoga Association; admission charged (prices not checked). From November to April it is in "Winter Quarters" and opens only on select dates for events and programmes listed on its calendar. Mount Defiance across the river, where the British put guns in 1777, is reached by road on the same ticket in season.',
  [W('Fort Ticonderoga'),FTH,L('Fort Ticonderoga: Mount Defiance','https://fortticonderoga.org/experience/explore-adirondacks/mount-defiance/')],brick='no',was='lm-fort-ticonderoga',
  hours='May 1 to October 31: Tuesday to Sunday 9:30 a.m. to 5 p.m.; November to April select dates only',
  th=[43.84243,-73.38869],thn='Fort Road lot'),
N('french-lines-carillon-battlefield','The French Lines and Carillon Battlefield','Ticonderoga','adk','fort','traces',43.84929,-73.39412,'site','open','walk','forts','1758–1777',
  'In July 1758 Montcalm\'s troops built a log wall across the ridge west of Fort Carillon in under two days, and behind it beat a British army that outnumbered them almost four to one. The French gave the lines up in 1759; the Americans rebuilt them in 1776–77 and named the ground Liberty Hill, and in September 1777 Colonel John Brown\'s men took them and briefly shelled the British in the fort. After the war the lines fell into ruin and are now a low earthen bank. Fort Ticonderoga calls them part of the most intact Revolutionary earthworks in America.',
  'The fort\'s loop trail (about 1.7 miles) passes the French Lines breastwork, two 1759 British battery sites, an 18th-century military road, the Great Redoubt the Americans built on the battlefield, a Massachusetts redoubt of 1777, the cellar holes of 1870s farm buildings, and the French Cross put up on August 21, 1758. The low bank of the lines can also be seen beside the fort\'s entrance road, where a state marker of 1932 stands. The fort asks visitors not to climb on the earthworks.',
  'The low earth bank where 4,000 French stopped 16,000 British.',
  'On Fort Ticonderoga\'s grounds; the trail is open June to October and is included in the fort\'s admission. Trail guides are at the guest services desk in the Log House Welcome Center. The markers are on the fort\'s road (Sandy Redoubt).',
  [L('Fort Ticonderoga: Carillon Battlefield hiking trail','https://fortticonderoga.org/experience/explore-adirondacks/carillon-battlefield/'),L('Fort Ticonderoga: Carillon Battlefield trail guide (PDF)','https://fortticonderoga.org/wp-content/uploads/2024/04/Carillon-Battlefield-Hiking-Trail-Guide-2024-1.pdf'),HM('126143','The French Lines & Carillon Battlefield'),HM('9565','The French Lines'),W('Battle of Carillon')],brick='no',
  hours='Trail June to October, on the fort\'s open days (Tuesday to Sunday 9:30 a.m. to 5 p.m.), with fort admission',
  th=[43.84406,-73.38462],thn='Sandy Redoubt lot'),
# ---- the lower falls and the old mill ground ----
N('ticonderoga-heritage-museum','Ticonderoga Heritage Museum (Ticonderoga Pulp and Paper Company office, 1888)','Ticonderoga','adk','industry','standing',43.84873,-73.42132,'exact','open','drive','','1888',
  'The Ticonderoga Pulp and Paper Company, organised in 1877 by Clayton H. Delano, built this two-storey brick office in 1888 and added a brick wing about 1910. The company\'s mills covered the ground round the lower falls of the La Chute River. International Paper, which came to control most of the water power, moved its Ticonderoga mill in 1971 and pulled down everything here except this "1888 building", the last structure of the mills. It is now the Ticonderoga Heritage Museum and the town\'s visitor centre.',
  'A brick office with grey slate gable roofs, white trim and a toothed brick cornice, on the edge of Bicentennial Park. Inside, the museum tells the story of the town\'s industries (contents not checked). The falls and the mill sites are a few steps away (another entry).',
  'The one building left of a paper mill that filled the falls.',
  'At the corner of Montcalm Street and Tower Avenue, by the town\'s Bicentennial Park. The town\'s page gives no museum hours. Lakes to Locks Passage\'s listing (last changed about 2019) gives free admission and 10 a.m. to 4 p.m., weekends from Memorial Day, daily from late June to Labor Day, then weekends to Columbus Day; not read from the museum\'s own page, so check before going. The park\'s rules bar all but authorised vehicles: park in the lot off Tower Avenue or on the street.',
  [W('Ticonderoga Pulp and Paper Company Office'),HMUS,L('Lakes to Locks Passage: Ticonderoga Heritage Museum','https://passageport.org/places-to-go/ticonderoga-heritage-museum/'),HM('125183','The Power of Water'),PARKS],brick='?',hours='?',
  th=[43.84823,-73.42263],thn='Lot off Tower Avenue (Depot Street)'),
N('bicentennial-falls-la-chute-mills','Bicentennial Falls: the lower falls of the La Chute and their mill sites','Ticonderoga','adk','industry','traces',43.84934,-73.42084,'site','public','walk','','1756–1971',
  'The La Chute River drops from Lake George to Lake Champlain, and at its last waterfall mills ran for more than two hundred years. Markers here say the French sawmill that cut the timber for Fort Carillon stood at these falls (1755 on one marker, 1756 on another), and the British used it in 1759 for their fleet and forts. Later came saw, pulp and paper, iron, woollen, cotton and graphite mills: the American Graphite Company, bought by Joseph Dixon\'s crucible company in 1873, refined graphite on the north bank from 1863 until its mill burned in 1968, when Dixon gave the land to the town. The paper mill came down after International Paper left in 1971 (one panel says 1970), and the ground became the town\'s Bicentennial Park in 1976.',
  'The falls themselves, seen from the riverwalk with its numbered history panels. Along the east side of Tower Avenue, just north of the museum, a row of old mill grindstones salvaged from the falls mills was set up in 1978. The town calls the park\'s covered footbridge the "Kissing Bridge". What else is left of the mills in the banks was not checked.',
  'Two centuries of mills on one waterfall, from the French sawmill to Dixon\'s graphite.',
  'Bicentennial Park is the town\'s, off Montcalm Street (Route 74) and Tower Avenue. The town bars vehicles and dogs from the park; park in the lot off Tower Avenue. The La Chute River Walk starts here (another entry). No park hours were given on the town\'s page.',
  [HM('125097','Rivière de La Chute'),HM('125183','The Power of Water'),HM('125185','Sharing the Flow'),HM('125100','Tower Avenue Grindstones'),PARKS],brick='no',
  th=[43.84823,-73.42263],thn='Lot off Tower Avenue (Depot Street)'),
N('frazier-bridge-ticonderoga','Frazier Bridge','Ticonderoga','adk','landmark','standing',43.85031,-73.42208,'exact','public','walk','','1894',
  'A double-arched masonry bridge over the La Chute River, 23 feet wide and spanning 50 feet. A bridge has crossed the river here since the lower falls were surveyed in 1822; it took its name from B. F. Frazier\'s planing mill of 1836. A cast-iron Cooper-patent bridge of 1874 collapsed in 1892, and in August 1894 the local paper reported Alex Lee hurrying work on the stone bridge. Wikipedia gives 1894; the town\'s own trail notes call it the Frazier Bridge of 1879, restored in 2008, so the year is not settled. It carried Main Street until 1960, when a building for the paper mill\'s No. 7 machine blocked the road and Tower Avenue became the crossing.',
  'The two arches with a cast-iron rail, seen from both ends of the La Chute River Walk. The town says it was restored in 2008.',
  '',
  'On the La Chute River Walk at the lower falls, near Bicentennial Park; the town\'s own. Park in the lot off Tower Avenue.',
  [W('Frazier Bridge'),FRAZ,HM('158199','Crossroads of a Community')],brick='?',conf='mixed',
  th=[43.84823,-73.42263],thn='Lot off Tower Avenue (Depot Street)'),
N('la-chute-upper-falls-mills','La Chute River Walk: the upper falls dams and mill sites at Alexandria','Ticonderoga','adk','industry','traces',43.83987,-73.42864,'area','public','walk','','1810–1960s',
  'The La Chute falls 99 feet in its first half mile out of Lake George, and the hamlet of Alexandria at the top was Ticonderoga\'s first settlement: by 1810 it had two forges, a tannery and sawmills. Guy Baldwin built the first "lead mill" for pencils here and filed his patent in 1835. A cotton mill followed in 1872, the Glens Falls Pulp Company built a brick and stone mill in 1891, and in 1893 the pulp mills merged as the Lake George Paper Company, one of the twenty that formed International Paper in 1898.',
  'The town says the river walk passes the old Island Mill property, Richards Dam, the "D" dam and the old Lake George Paper Company "B" mill, then the "A" mill site near Alexandria Avenue, and that at the "C" dam site water still falls over some of the remaining stones. The town counts six waterfalls on the river. Which pieces of the mills still stand was not checked; the pin is the history panel on Lake George Avenue.',
  'The top of a river that fell 99 feet in half a mile, and the mills that used every foot.',
  'The La Chute River Walk is the town\'s trail, about three miles from Lake George to Lake Champlain, starting in Bicentennial Park. The panel is beside Lake George Avenue north of Highland Street. Parking along this end not checked.',
  [HM('314052','Upper Falls Industry'),PARKS,WALK],brick='?',conf='mixed'),
